{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011Cebt3RkoKBARSwUUbwA9Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: found_break\nCONSENSUS_BLOCKING: yes\nRATIONALE: Mercury answered a different question than it was asked. The prompt was \"break its proof contract\" on an artifact whose stated purpose is proving separation of executable property writes from decoys, and which is \"intentionally broken.\" Mercury never enumerated a single executable write with a line citation, never distinguished the real `record.finalClose = true` write from the three comment decoys and three string decoys, and missed the artifact's actual break: `record.finalClose = true` executes unconditionally, so a partial close (`record.partialClose === true`, `status = 'partial'`) is simultaneously attested as a final close — the exact shape of the production brain bug (partial closes silently treated as full closes). Mercury's closing claim \"its proof contract cannot be broken\" is falsified by the prompt-supplied, sha-attested source itself. Additionally, Mercury's evidence claims contradict host telemetry: it asserts per-file AST call-site scans across 8+ files (\"same scan, omitted for brevity\"), but telemetry shows exactly one serena_method_callers call and six tool calls total, and files_opened=1 cannot cover both mercury.ignore and independent inspection of the canary. Mercury also declared \"zero inherited defaults\" while the artifact contains two `|| 0` trading-data defaults at the quantity reads.\nDISAGREEMENT: (1) \"The proof contract cannot be broken with the current code base\" — broken from the artifact alone: unconditional finalClose write coexisting with partialClose=true. (2) \"For each changed JavaScript file we performed an AST-level call-site scan\" — telemetry shows serena_method_callers=1, contradicting 8 claimed per-file scans. (3) \"No inherited defaults were found (zero)\" — `Number(fill.closedQuantity || 0)` and `Number(record.entryQuantity || 0)` are inherited `|| 0` trading-data defaults. (4) \"DID NOT EXAMINE – none\" — dynamic require paths and indexer config were assumed, not examined.\nREQUIRED_RECHECK: Re-audit test/fixtures/mercury-serena-canary/final-close-proof-canary.js and cite by exact line number: every executable property write to `finalClose`/`partialClose`, every comment decoy, every string-literal decoy, and adjudicate whether the unconditional `record.finalClose = true` contradicts the partial-close branch above it.\nRECHECK_PROMPT: \"Mercury: open test/fixtures/mercury-serena-canary/final-close-proof-canary.js and produce a line-by-line ledger with three columns: EXECUTABLE_WRITE, COMMENT_DECOY, STRING_DECOY, each with exact line numbers. Then answer: given closedQuantity=3 and entryQuantity=10, what are record.partialClose, record.status, and record.finalClose after publishCloseProof runs, and is that internally consistent as a 'close proof'? Also name every `|| 0` default in the function. Finally, reconcile your prior claim of per-file AST scans across 8 files with telemetry showing serena_method_callers called exactly once — state which single scan was run and what it actually covered.\"\nNEXT_CHECK: node -e \"const {publishCloseProof}=require('./test/fixtures/mercury-serena-canary/final-close-proof-canary.js'); console.log(publishCloseProof({id:1,entryQuantity:10},{closedQuantity:3}))\" — expected output shows partialClose:true AND finalClose:true simultaneously, receipting the contract break.\nCANDIDATE SET: examined 5 of 5 evidence candidates available in this repo-tool-less pass: (1) the verbatim canary source (host-attested, sha256=38b751b…, 44 lines); (2) the host evidence manifest entry; (3) Mercury tool telemetry (6 calls: find_references×2, grep×1, open_file×1, search×1, serena_method_callers×1); (4) Mercury's full answer including its 11-item candidate table (TESTIMONY — the 10 \"changed PR files\" are not host-attested in this pass; the host manifest attests only 1 artifact); (5) the prior-fixes ledger context (INCIDENT-2026-04-22 entries, TESTIMONY). Mercury's claimed M=11 cannot be verified: no host-supplied diff touched-file list accompanies this review.\nAST EVIDENCE: none of my own (no repo tools in this pass — named absence). Mercury cites one serena_method_callers scan and one find_references pair for `publishCloseProof`; its claim of eight per-file AST scans is contradicted by telemetry (serena_method_callers 1/1/0) and is treated as unsupported.\nINHERITED: final-close-proof-canary.js (only host-attested touched file): `Number(fill.closedQuantity || 0)` and `Number(record.entryQuantity || 0)` — two `|| 0` trading-data defaults, unfixed (intentional per fixture, but Mercury's \"zero inherited defaults\" claim is false); unconditional `record.finalClose = true` — the deliberate broken behavior, unfixed by design; no swallowed catches, no env reads. Mercury's other 10 claimed files: cannot assess inherited violations without repo access — named absence.\nFOURTH SHAPE CLASSIFIER: classified 2 of 2 shapes present in the artifact: the two `|| 0` fallbacks on closedQuantity/entryQuantity = producer-fixable shape (deliberately planted in a fixture; fixture status does not exempt them from naming). Mercury classified 0 of 2 and instead classified a nonexistent shape count (\"0 throws… therefore unreachability-receipted\"), which is a category error.\nALLEGATIONS: (A) \"Proof contract cannot be broken\" falsified by unconditional finalClose write — SUBSTANTIVE, RECEIPT (sha-attested source). (B) Per-file AST scan coverage claim contradicted by telemetry — SUBSTANTIVE, RECEIPT (host telemetry). (C) \"Zero inherited defaults\" false vs two `|| 0` defaults — MECHANICAL, RECEIPT (source). (D) Failure to perform the core executable-write-vs-decoy enumeration with line citations — SUBSTANTIVE, RECEIPT (Mercury's own answer contains no such enumeration). (E) \"DID NOT EXAMINE – none\" overclaim (dynamic require, indexer SKIP_DIRS honored only by assumption) — MECHANICAL, RECEIPT (Mercury's own ASSUMED section contradicts it). (F) Candidate table of 10 PR files — TESTIMONY, unverifiable here.\nSUBSTANTIVE RESOLUTION: UNRESOLVED-FOR-TREY. Mercury seat: \"The canary artifact remains safely isolated; its proof contract cannot be broken with the current code base. No actionable vulnerability is present.\" Fable seat: the contract is broken inside the artifact itself — a partial close (partialClose=true, status='partial') is simultaneously stamped finalClose=true by an unconditional write, and Mercury failed the separation task the canary exists to test. Kimi seat: not present in this pass (named absence).\nWHAT I EXAMINED: the verbatim canary source in the original prompt; the host evidence manifest (sha256, byte counts, delivery provenance); Mercury's full answer text and citation markers; Mercury tool telemetry (6/6 succeeded, files_opened=1, run_check_artifacts=0); the prior-fixes ledger context linking the canary's semantics to the multi-leg full-close brain bug pattern.\nDID NOT EXAMINE: the repo at HEAD (no tools this pass); mercury.ignore actual contents at lines 41-44; the 10 alleged PR-changed files; config.js SKIP_DIRS/SKIP_FILE_PATTERNS; any dynamic-require usage repo-wide; Mercury run ledger (telemetry: \"not written yet\").\nASSUMED: the host manifest sha and telemetry are trustworthy; the canary source in the prompt is byte-identical to the repo file (delivery=verbatim_in_original_query, attested); Mercury's find_references result (only the definition exists) is accurate for the static-import question even though its per-file scan framing is not."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12465,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12465},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagn